Description: Establishes a deterministic geometric extension framework 
    within the 'theaitetos' package, honoring the ancient mathematical 
    foundations of Theaetetus of Athens (400 BC). Incorporates the 
    stereometric proofs on the regular octahedron later recorded in 
    Plato (ca. 369 BC) "Theaetetus" <urn:cts:greekLit:tlg0059.tlg006> and 
    Euclid (ca. 300 BC) "Elements" <urn:cts:greekLit:tlg1799.tlg001>. 
    References the standard Greek text tradition by Heiberg (1885) 
    "Euclidis Elementa" and the analytical translations by Heath (1926) 
    "The Thirteen Books of Euclid's Elements". To achieve operational 
    utility, the architecture delivers a high-level computational core 
    engine that abstracts complex 3D-to-2D vector projections and scalar 
    wave superpositions, eliminating low-level reprogramming. It 
    integrates a standardized visualization layer built upon 'ggplot2' 
    to render high-resolution continuous scalar fields and provides a 
    runtime-configurable selection interface across multiple physical 
    and biological dimensions. Operates under the modern paradigm of a 
    hypothetical holographic universe by referencing the foundational 
    concepts of 't Hooft (1993) <doi:10.48550/arXiv.gr-qc/9310026> and 
    Susskind (1995) <doi:10.1063/1.531249> to project Euclidean 
    invariants onto a lower-dimensional boundary plane. Serves as a 
    creative epistemological sub-module utilizing the core analytical 
    computations of the 'octawave' package.
